{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01MpHmskDeQHM35Pk9pN4kfN","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Translation conventions (MuJS C -> Rust cdylib)\n2\t\n3\tGoal: byte-identical behavior with the C library. Faithful transliteration, not\n4\ta rewrite. Do NOT fix bugs. Preserve order of operations, error checks, and all\n5\tobservable output exactly.\n6\t\n7\t## Shared types\n8\tAll shared types/consts live in `crate::types` (see src/types.rs). Import with\n9\t`use crate::types::*;`. Key points:\n10\t- `js_State`, `js_Object`, `js_Value` (a `#[repr(C)]` union), `js_Property`,\n11\t  `js_Function`, `js_Ast`, `js_Environment`, `js_Regexp`, `js_Buffer`, etc.\n12\t- `js_Value` union access: `v.t.type_` is the type tag (a `c_char`). Value\n13\t  fields: `v.u.boolean`, `v.u.number`, `v.u.litstr`, `v.u.memstr`, `v.u.object`,\n14\t  `v.u.shrstr`. Use `js_Value::zeroed()` to make a zeroed value.\n15\t- `js_Object.type_` is the class (JS_COBJECT..). Object union: `(*o).u.number`,\n16\t  `(*o).u.boolean`, `(*o).u.s` (ObjS), `(*o).u.a` (ObjA), `(*o).u.f` (ObjF),\n17\t  `(*o).u.c` (ObjC), `(*o).u.r` (js_Regexp), `(*o).u.iter` (ObjIter),\n18\t  `(*o).u.user` (ObjUser). Note C field `delete` -> Rust field `delete` (ok).\n19\t- Enum tags are plain `pub const ... : c_int` (or `c_char` for value types).\n20\t- `#define foo NAMESPACE(foo)` etc: the LINKER symbol names are defined in\n21\t  regexp.h/utf.h. Exported symbol must match the final name.\n22\t\n23\t## Function signatures & exports\n24\t- Every function that is a public/linker symbol (see nm list) MUST be\n25\t  `#[no_mangle] pub unsafe extern \"C-unwind\" fn NAME(...) -> ...`.\n26\t  Use `#[no_mangle]` (crate uses edition 2021; write `#[no_mangle]`).\n27\t- Internal/static C functions become `pub(crate) unsafe fn` (NOT no_mangle) so\n28\t  other modules can call them. Keep the same name (prefix module if collision).\n29\t- Use C types from `std::os::raw`: `c_char, c_int, c_uint, c_ushort, c_double`(=f64),\n30\t  `c_void`. Pointers: `*const c_char`, `*mut js_State`, etc.\n31\t- All functions are `unsafe` and operate on raw pointers, exactly like C.\n32\t\n33\t## Calling other modules\n34\tCross-module functions are referenced as `crate::MODULE::func(...)`. A registry\n35\tof who-defines-what is in the C headers (jsi.h/mujs.h/regexp.h/utf.h). Common:\n36\t- memory/stack/run: `crate::jsrun::*` (js_malloc, js_realloc, js_free, js_pushX,\n37\t  js_pop, stackidx, js_call, js_throw, ...)\n38\t- values: `crate::jsvalue::*` (jsV_tostring, jsV_tonumber, js_itoa, ...)\n39\t- properties: `crate::jsproperty::*` (jsV_newobject, jsV_getproperty, ...)\n40\t- intern/buffer: `crate::jsintern::*` (js_intern, js_putc, js_puts, js_putm,\n41\t  js_strdup, jsS_freestrings)\n42\t- lexer: `crate::jslex::*`; parser: `crate::jsparse::*`; compiler:`crate::jscompile::*`\n43\t- utf: `crate::utf::*` (jsU_chartorune etc; but call via the short names too)\n44\t- dtoa: `crate::jsdtoa::*` (js_strtod, js_grisu2, js_fmtexp)\n45\t- regexp: `crate::regexp::*` (js_regcomp, js_regexec, js_regfree, js_regcompx, js_regfreex)\n46\t- errors: `crate::jserror::*` (js_typeerror, js_rangeerror, js_throw is in jsrun)\n47\tExact ownership is given per-task. If unsure where a symbol lives, grep the C\n48\theaders. The public API (js_* from mujs.h) lives across jsrun/jsvalue/jsstate/etc\n49\tas in the C sources.\n50\t\n51\t## Exceptions (setjmp/longjmp)\n52\tC uses `if (js_try(J)) { handler; } body; js_endtry(J);`. Translate this pattern\n53\tusing `crate::except::protect`:\n54\t\n55\t```\n56\tif crate::except::protect(J, || {\n57\t    // body up to js_endtry (do NOT call js_endtry inside; protect handles the\n58\t    // frame accounting via savetry, and body must NOT call js_endtry — instead\n59\t    // the normal js_endtry that appears AFTER the C body is emitted by having\n60\t    // body end right before it, then we call js_endtry after protect returns false)\n61\t}) {\n62\t    // C handler block (state already restored, exception value on stack)\n63\t    // e.g. js_free(J, sb); js_throw(J);\n64\t} else {\n65\t    // frame completed normally: call js_endtry(J) here, then continue\n66\t}\n67\t```\n68\t\n69\tIMPORTANT detail: In C, the sequence is:\n70\t```\n71\tif (js_try(J)) { HANDLER }   // setjmp\n72\tBODY\n73\tjs_endtry(J);\n74\tREST\n75\t```\n76\tTranslate as:\n77\t```\n78\tlet caught = crate::except::protect(J, || { BODY });\n79\tif caught { HANDLER } else { crate::jsrun::js_endtry(J); }\n80\tREST\n81\t```\n82\tNote BODY must not early-return out of the closure in a way that skips endtry;\n83\tuse the closure to run exactly the statements between setjmp and js_endtry.\n84\tFor functions where the handler ends in `js_throw(J)` (re-throw) that's fine —\n85\tjs_throw panics and unwinds further.\n86\t\n87\t`js_throw` (in jsrun) pops trybuf, restores E/envtop/tracetop/top/bot/strict,\n88\tpushes the value, and `panic`s with `JsThrow{target}`. If trytop==0 it calls\n89\tpanic handler and aborts.\n90\t\n91\tThe main interpreter loop `jsR_run` handles OP_TRY by using `protect` around the\n92\tremainder — but jsR_run is provided/owned by the jsrun task; other modules only\n93\tuse `protect` for their local `js_try` sites.\n94\t\n95\t## Numbers / formatting\n96\t- `NAN`, `INFINITY`: use `f64::NAN`, `f64::INFINITY`. `isnan`->`x.is_nan()`,\n97\t  `isinf`->`x.is_infinite()`, `isfinite`->`x.is_finite()`, `signbit`->`x.is_sign_negative()`,\n98\t  `floor/ceil/fabs/fmod/pow/...`-> f64 methods or libc.\n99\t- `sprintf`/`snprintf`/`vsnprintf` with format strings: use `libc::snprintf`\n100\t  via a fixed stack buffer to keep byte-identical printf semantics (%g, %e, %f,\n101\t  %d, %04d, %+d, %p, %02d:%02d etc). Build the format C-string as a byte literal\n102\t  `b\"...\\0\"`. Do NOT reimplement float formatting in Rust — call libc.\n103\t- integer casts follow C truncation semantics: `x as c_int` on f64 in Rust is a\n104\t  saturating cast, NOT C's UB truncation. When C does `(int)double`, and the\n105\t  value is known in range it's fine; where C relies on wrap/truncation for\n106\t  in-range values it's equivalent. Match code paths exactly as written.\n107\t\n108\t## String buffers & C strings\n109\t- C string literals: use `b\"...\\0\".as_ptr() as *const c_char`. Define a helper\n110\t  `cstr!` pattern inline, or `b\"foo\\0\".as_ptr() as *const c_char`.\n111\t- Character access `s[i]`: `*s.add(i)` (as c_char). Cast to unsigned via `as u8`.\n112\t- `*s++` idioms: track a pointer `let mut s = ...; let c = *s; s = s.add(1);`.\n113\t\n114\t## Static/global C data\n115\t- `static const` tables -> `static` arrays or `const` arrays in Rust.\n116\t- Function-local `static` mutable (e.g. LocalTZA once/tza, stackidx undefined) ->\n117\t  use a `static mut` or a module-level cache; preserve exact behavior.\n118\t- The property AA-tree `sentinel` and intern `sentinel` are shared module\n119\t  statics — owned by jsproperty and jsintern respectively; expose via a getter.\n120\t\n121\t## Don'ts\n122\t- Don't reorder checks. Don't add bounds checks C doesn't have. Don't \"fix\" UB.\n123\t- Don't use Rust std collections for engine data; use the raw C structures.\n124\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01PZsdJaJ2XAu9taN4hdmAiA","session_id":"d89652df-3211-45b0-b03a-84321fcd3e8b","uuid":"1d95715c-3a9b-401b-9dfe-dd04443679a5","timestamp":"2026-08-05T04:23:31.347Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Translate jsboolean.c to Rust"}
